      "text": "$AB=CP, AC(DE+PI)=AC(ID)+CP(BI)$\n$AC(DE+IP)=AB(BI)+AC(ID)$\n$\\angle BCP=PDE$\n$AP\\sqrt{BC\\sin\\bigg(2\\angle ACP\\bigg)}PM=AP\\sqrt{BC}\\sin\\bigg(2\\angle ACP\\bigg)PM=AP\\sqrt{BC}(2PM\\sin(45^\\circ-\\angle BAC)-PM\\frac{\\sin(45^\\circ+\\angle BAC)}{2})=$\n\n\nHello, please reveal your method.\n\nEdit: Can someone remove my LaTeX?\n\nWithout loss of generality, rotate the diagram 90 degrees clockwise. Let $P'$ be the point $P$ will rotate to. Then, because $\\angle APC = 2\\angle ACP$, $$\\angle APP' = 2 \\angle ACP.$$Thus, the area of $\\triangle APP'$ is $4$ times the area of $\\triangle APC$; i.e. $$\\frac12 (AP')\\left(\\frac12 AP\\right) = 4 \\frac12 AP\\left(\\frac12 CP\\right).$$Solving for $\\frac{AP'}{BP'}$, and looking at ratio with the given condition, gives $$\\frac{AP'}{AP}=3-2\\sqrt2.$$\n\nUsing the same reference problem:\n\nhttps://www.artofproblemsolving.com/community/c1304/a2_32nd_year_auburn_2022_problem_4",
