      "text": "Given:\n- \\( \\triangle ABC \\) is a right triangle with \\( \\angle C = 90^\\circ \\).\n- \\( \\angle BAC < 45^\\circ \\).\n- \\( AB = 4 \\).\n- Point \\( P \\) on \\( \\overline{AB} \\) such that \\( \\angle APC = 2 \\angle ACP \\) and \\( CP = 1 \\).\n\nWe want to find the ratio \\( \\frac{AP}{BP} \\).\n\n1. **Similarity and Parallelogram Traps:**\n   Without loss of generality (W.L.G.), let us place the triangle in the coordinate plane such that:\n   - Point \\( C \\) is at the origin, \\( C = (0, 0) \\).\n   - Point \\( A \\) is at \\( (a, 0) \\).\n   - Point \\( B \\) is at \\( (0, b) \\).\n\n2. **Finding Coordinates with \\( \\angle ACP = \\theta \\):**\n   Since \\( \\angle ACP = \\theta \\), the slope of \\( PC \\) (which is also the slope of \\( AP \\)) is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{slope of } PC = \\tan \\theta = \\frac{1}{\\tan \\theta} = \\frac{a}{1} = a\n   \\]\n   Knowing \\( AB = 4 \\), the coordinates of \\( P \\) being \\( (x, y) \\) would satisfy the equation of the hypotenuse:\n   \\[\n   y = -ax + 4a + b\n   \\]\n\n3. **Setting Up the Angles:**\n   We know:\n   - \\( CP \\) to meet the number conditions in the problem.\n   The additional conditions of the angles distract us from finding the direct relationship at the start but lead to auxiliary calculations:\n\n   Indeed, chasing using the given problem conditions gives:\n   - \\( \\angle ACP + \\angle APC = 2 \\angle ACP \\):\n   \\[\n   \\tan \\theta + \\frac{1}{\\tan \\theta} = 2 \\tan \\theta\n   \\]\n   Simplifies without holing both slopes in wanted terms as:\n   \\boxed{3} and \\( q = 2, r = 5 \\):\n\n   Hence, the ratio \\( \\frac{AP}{BP} \\) is represented in \\( 3 + 2\\sqrt{5} \\):\n\n4. Summing \\( p, q \\) and \\( r \\):\n   \\[\n   p + q + r = 3 + 2 + 5 = 10\n   \\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{10}\n\\]",
